From 339bfbe9d8424bed1e9de6195e0fe1de44afe143 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 06:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayerVideo: the repeated field of a picture lengthens
 it, it does not delay its start

MPEG-2 (repeat_first_field), H.264 (pic_struct) and VC-1 show the extra field
of a picture after its other fields: the picture starts at its own time and
lasts longer, and only the next one is later. ProcessDecoderOutput() added
the extra time to the duration and also to the picture's time, so a picture
with a repeated field started half a picture late (20.85 ms at 23.976).

In a Matroska file of a soft telecined film that declares 23.976 and keeps
the flags, every second picture had its time 21 ms late; on a 23.976 Hz
display one picture was then shown twice and the one before it not at all in
half of the phases of the display (steps of 21/62 ms between pictures
instead of 42/41). RepeatedFieldTiming() (RepeatedField.h, no dependencies)
gives the time and duration; a positive repeat leaves the start alone. The
-0.5 that DVDVideoCodecFFmpeg gives a picture whose time repeats the one
before it keeps its shift.

Measured with the timing path of VideoPlayerVideo replayed on the pictures
that Kodi's demuxer and FFmpeg 9.0.2's decoder give for soft telecined
MPEG-2 (2400 film pictures, as a DVD title, a VOB, a .m2v and as Matroska):
Matroska with uniform times, declared 23.976: 2-0 refreshes per picture in
2 of 4 phases -> 1-1 in all 4 (steps 42/41 ms); with 3:2 times of the disc:
one phase of 2-0 becomes one of 0-2 (the cadence needs the detection of the
soft telecine, a later change); DVD, VOB and .m2v: 59.94 Hz with 3-2-3-2
becomes 2-3-2-3 (the order that MPEG-2 gives), the same judder. The pictures
shown are the same.

Not measured: H.264 pic_struct and VC-1 (no encoder for them on the host);
the rule is the same for them. A host test covers the header (6 groups of
checks; three mutants of the header fail it).
